# P09.33 — v1 naming cleanup: shorter public attribute names
# and macro renames.
#
# Public-facing rename pass. Shortens the five user-facing
# attributes and the `cxx_class_native!` macro to names that
# read naturally in user code. Internal attributes
# (`rustc_cxx_wrapper`, `rustc_cxx_drop_wrapper`,
# `rustc_cxx_base`) are left unchanged — users never write them.
#
# # Renames
#
# | Old | New |
# |---|---|
# | `#[rustc_cxx_ctor]`     | `#[constructor]` |
# | `#[rustc_cxx_virtual]`  | `#[cpp_virtual]` |
# | `#[rustc_cxx_operator]` | `#[operator]` |
# | `#[rustc_swift_type]`   | `#[swift_type]` |
# | `#[rustc_swift_symbol]` | `#[swift_symbol]` |
# | `cxx_class_native!`     | `native_cpp_class!` |
#
# # Naming rationale
#
# - `constructor` — self-documenting, no collision with the
#   `ctor` crate on crates.io.
# - `cpp_virtual` — the `virtual` identifier alone is a reserved
#   Rust keyword (can't be used as `#[virtual]` directly); the
#   `cpp_` prefix disambiguates and matches the repr(cpp)
#   naming convention.
# - `operator` — no collisions; the attribute only applies to
#   methods on `#[repr(cpp)]` impls so its scope is unambiguous.
# - `swift_type` / `swift_symbol` — `#[type]` alone would be a
#   parse error (reserved keyword). The `swift_` prefix keeps
#   parity with the ABI these attrs target.
# - `native_cpp_class!` — "native C++ class" reads better in
#   English than "C++ native class"; also avoids semantic
#   overlap with the `cpp` crate's `cpp_class!` macro.
#
# # What's not renamed
#
# - `rustc_cxx_wrapper`, `rustc_cxx_drop_wrapper`,
#   `rustc_cxx_base` — these are emitted internally by the
#   `native_cpp_class!` macro (and the `class` keyword's parser
#   desugar) to mark methods / fields for compiler paths. Users
#   don't write them, so shortening is cosmetic churn.
# - `cxx_class!` — stable-rustc-compatible macro, untouched.
# - `swift_value!` — macro name unchanged; only its *emission*
#   updates to the new `#[swift_type]` attribute name.
# - `#[repr(cpp)]`, `#[repr(swift)]`, `extern "C++"`,
#   `extern "Swift"` — these are the fundamental type-system
#   and ABI markers, not rename candidates.
#
# # Files touched
#
# Compiler (4 files):
#
# - `rustc_span/src/symbol.rs` — add `cpp_virtual`, `operator`,
#   `swift_symbol`, `swift_type` syms. Drop `rustc_cxx_ctor`,
#   `rustc_cxx_virtual`, `rustc_cxx_operator`,
#   `rustc_swift_type`, `rustc_swift_symbol`. `constructor`
#   was already interned elsewhere — reused, not duplicated.
# - `rustc_feature/src/builtin_attrs.rs` — update 5
#   `rustc_attr!` entries.
# - `rustc_attr_parsing/src/attributes/codegen_attrs.rs` —
#   update 5 `PATH` fields on the parser structs. The
#   `AttributeKind` variant names (`RustcCxxCtor`,
#   `RustcCxxVirtual`, etc.) stay unchanged; they're internal
#   compiler identifiers that don't cross the API boundary.
# - (symbol table ripple effects compile-checked at every stage.)
#
# Workspace (1 file):
#
# - `crates/rustcc_macros/src/lib.rs` — proc macro renamed
#   (`pub fn cxx_class_native` → `pub fn native_cpp_class`).
#   Macro-emitted attribute names all updated. `swift_value!`
#   emission updated from `#[rustc_swift_type]` to
#   `#[swift_type]`.
